findings.finding:
My current finding is that **only cyclotomic polynomials with even index can appear in a divisor of a binomial \(1+x^{d}\)**.  In fact, writing \(d=2^{r}d_{0}\) \((d_{0}\) odd\()\) gives  

\[
1+x^{d}= \prod_{f\mid d_{0}}\Phi_{2^{\,r+1}f}(x),
\]

so every factor \(\Phi_{n}\) has \(v_{2}(n)=r+1\ge1\).  Consequently \(\Phi_{1}(x)=x-1\) never occurs, and the admissible cyclotomics are exactly those \(\Phi_{n}\) with even \(n\) and \(\varphi(n)\le8\):  

\[
\Phi_{2}=x+1,\;\Phi_{4}=x^{2}+1,\;\Phi_{6}=x^{2}-x+1,\;\Phi_{8}=x^{4}+1,\;
\Phi_{10}=x^{4}-x^{3}+x^{2}-x+1,\;
\Phi_{12}=x^{4}-x^{2}+1,\;
\Phi_{14}=x^{6}-x^{5}+x^{4}-x^{3}+x^{2}-x+1,\;
\Phi_{16}=x^{8}+1.
\]

Any admissible \(\tilde F(x)\) must be a signed product of a **subset of these cyclotomics that all share the same 2‑adic exponent** (i.e. the same \(v_{2}(n)\)).  The final count of shifty functions is obtained by enumerating, for each monomial factor \(x^{p}\;(0\le p\le8)\), all such subsets whose total degree \(\le8-p\) (including the empty set, which gives \(\pm x^{p}\)).  This corrects earlier claims that \(\Phi_{1},\Phi_{3},\Phi_{5},\Phi_{7}\) could appear.
